<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samsi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Shamsi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samsi was a queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in the 8th century BCE, known from the inscriptions of Tiglath-Pileser III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>. She succeeded Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabibe">Zabibe</a>, broke her oath of loyalty and sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, but Tiglath-Pileser defeated her around 733 BCE, taking captives and great herds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a>, and left her on the throne under an Assyrian official. She later paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Šamši came to power as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> when the former queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zabibe">Zabibe</a> abdicated in her favour. On taking the throne she renewed Zabibe's oath of allegiance, swearing by the sun, the god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, to be loyal to Assyria, and Tiglath-Pileser III, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-nirari-v">Ashur-nirari V</a>, formally recognised her accession. He was the first foreign ruler to bring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> under his control. Like the queens before and after her, Šamši led embassies and caravans carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Assyrian chronicles describe her as a powerful ruler, bold enough to face the Assyrian kings in the 730s and 720s. She broke her oath by joining the alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rezin">Rezin of Damascus</a> against Tiglath-Pileser. The Assyrian army, having recently subdued Edom, turned on the anti-Assyrian forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and defeated her near Mount Sa-qu-ur-ri, a place not identified. According to the Assyrian records the victors took many prisoners, 30,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and more than 20,000 oxen; an inscription adds that 9,400 of her soldiers were killed and that 5,000 bags of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, the altars of her gods, arms including an ornamental staff of her goddess, and her estates were seized.</p><p>The chroniclers wrote that she fled the battlefield like a wild she-ass of the desert, and Tiglath-Pileser burned the tents left behind. She was soon captured, or by another account escaped to the land of Bazu and then surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> restored her because they needed a pliable Arab ruler to keep open the profitable trade route running north and south from Assyrian territory across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Tiglath-Pileser set a qepu, or governor, over her and her 10,000 soldiers, and she paid a tribute of gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camels</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>. Seven other kingdoms of the Arabian trade, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massa-tuscany">Massa</a>, Tyma and Saba, also had to pay tribute and guard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> trade. Šamši ruled for over 20 years, and Queen Iatie succeeded her in about 700 BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Šamši” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Queen Zabibe paid tribute to Tiglath-Pileser III in 738 BCE, followed by Queen <b>Samsi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/qedarites">Qedarites</a></li></ul></section>
